{
  "id": "m03-signed-mod-ops",
  "title": "Sum and Product Modulo a Prime",
  "source": {
    "name": "Original exercise on modular arithmetic with negative numbers"
  },
  "rating": 900,
  "tags": [
    "math",
    "modular-arithmetic"
  ],
  "statement": "You are given `n` integers `a_1, ..., a_n`, some of them negative. Let `p = 1 000 000 007`.\n\nPrint two numbers separated by a space: the sum `a_1 + ... + a_n` modulo `p`, and the product `a_1 * ... * a_n` modulo `p`. Both answers must lie in the range `[0, p - 1]`, even when the true sum or product is negative.\n\n**Input.** First line: `n`. Second line: the `n` integers.\n\n**Output.** One line: `sum product`.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 ≤ n ≤ 2000",
    "−10^18 ≤ a_i ≤ 10^18"
  ],
  "timeLimitMs": 2000,
  "hints": [
    "In C++ and Java the operator % keeps the sign of the left operand, so -7 % 5 is -2. First normalise each number into [0, p-1].",
    "Reduce each a_i with ((a_i % p) + p) % p (or Math.floorMod in Java), then fold the numbers in one at a time, reducing after every operation.",
    "A product of two residues is below about 10^18, which fits in a signed 64-bit integer, but a product of many residues does not. Reduce after every single multiplication. In JavaScript use BigInt because 10^18 is beyond 2^53."
  ],
  "editorial": "Work with residues the whole time. Normalise each input with `v = ((a % p) + p) % p`, then update `sum = (sum + v) % p` and `prod = prod * v % p`.\n\nThree traps are tested: negative inputs (a plain `%` can return a negative value in C++/Java), a product that overflows 64 bits if you forget to reduce after each step, and inputs near 10^18 that do not fit a JavaScript `Number` exactly. Python has none of these problems because its integers are unbounded and its `%` result takes the sign of the divisor.",
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(1)"
  },
  "starter": {
    "python": "import sys\nP = 10**9 + 7\ndata = sys.stdin.read().split()\nn = int(data[0])\na = list(map(int, data[1:1 + n]))\n# print: sum mod P, product mod P (both in [0, P-1])\n",
    "javascript": "// Use BigInt: values reach 10^18.\nconst n = Number(readLine());\nconst a = readLine().trim().split(/\\s+/).map(BigInt);\nconst P = 1000000007n;\n// your code here\n",
    "cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    cin >> n;\n    const long long P = 1000000007LL;\n    // read n values, print sum and product mod P\n}\n",
    "java": "import java.util.*;\nimport java.io.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(new String(System.in.readAllBytes()));\n        int n = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());\n        final long P = 1_000_000_007L;\n        // read n values, print sum and product mod P\n    }\n}\n"
  },
  "solutions": {
    "python": "import sys\nP = 10**9 + 7\ndata = sys.stdin.read().split()\nn = int(data[0])\ns, pr = 0, 1\nfor x in data[1:1 + n]:\n    v = int(x) % P\n    s = (s + v) % P\n    pr = pr * v % P\nprint(s, pr)\n",
    "javascript": "const n = Number(readLine());\nconst a = readLine().trim().split(/\\s+/).map(BigInt);\nconst P = 1000000007n;\nlet s = 0n, pr = 1n;\nfor (const x of a) {\n  const v = ((x % P) + P) % P;\n  s = (s + v) % P;\n  pr = (pr * v) % P;\n}\nconsole.log(s + ' ' + pr);\n",
    "cpp": "#include <bits/stdc++.h>\nusing namespace std;\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    cin >> n;\n    const long long P = 1000000007LL;\n    long long s = 0, pr = 1;\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n        long long x;\n        cin >> x;\n        long long v = ((x % P) + P) % P;\n        s = (s + v) % P;\n        pr = pr * v % P;\n    }\n    cout << s << \" \" << pr << endl;\n}\n",
    "java": "import java.util.*;\nimport java.io.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {\n        StringTokenizer st = new StringTokenizer(new String(System.in.readAllBytes()));\n        int n = Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());\n        final long P = 1_000_000_007L;\n        long s = 0, pr = 1;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            long v = Math.floorMod(Long.parseLong(st.nextToken()), P);\n            s = (s + v) % P;\n            pr = pr * v % P;\n        }\n        System.out.println(s + \" \" + pr);\n    }\n}\n"
  },
